Developer's Diary:

Migrating from Vite to Next.js felt less like a rewrite and more like a shift in mental model — instead of thinking about one bundle 
that runs entirely in the browser, you start thinking about which parts of the app need to run on the server and which need to run 
on the client. The biggest conceptual shift was understanding server components: the About page became a pure server component with 
no "use client" directive, which means it renders to HTML on the server at build time and ships zero JavaScript to the browser for 
that page. The search page stayed a client component because it needs useState, useSearchParams, and TanStack Query hooks. Setting up 
next-intl was the most configuration-heavy part — it required a middleware file, routing config, request config, translation JSON files, 
and a createNavigation wrapper, but once everything connected the language switcher worked instantly. The CSV generation moved from 
browser APIs (Blob, URL.createObjectURL) to a server route handler that returns a proper HTTP response with Content-Type: text/csv 
headers, which is cleaner and more secure. The hardest debugging moment was the 404 on /en which turned out to be a missing page.tsx 
file in the [locale] folder — Next.js file-based routing is unforgiving about file placement.


